The only difference is that, in Helix, the server sends an [insertReplaceEdit](https://microsoft.github.io/language-server-protocol/specifications/lsp/3.18/specification/#insertReplaceEdit) in "textEdit" field, because the client provided `textDocument.completion.completionItem.insertReplaceSupport` capability.

The simplest solution would be for Neovim to support this capability as well. That way, the server could compute the ranges itself, and client wouldn't need to account different ranges for each filtered completion item. However, the current response doesn't violate the LSP specification and should still be supported.

The main issue seems to be that `complete-items` doesn't allow specifying different `startcol` values per item. A possible workaround would be to prepend the missing prefix to all completion items so they align with the leftmost start position. In this case, that would result in the items `{ ".callee", ".length", "[Symbol]" }`.

> [@participant2](https://github.com/participant2) I had a bit more time to look at what you're saying with regards to the symbol response from typescript-language-server, and how it impacts the prefix and therefore the completion menu items. I decided to just try a simple test
> 
> ```
> const x = [1,2,3]
> x. <-- select "Symbol" from the completion menu
> x[Symbol] <-- result! 
> ```

This works because tsserver returns completion labels exactly as `{ ".callee", ".length", "[Symbol]" }` (with "." prefix) in this case.

@reporter the server get the current cursor position and can avoid include (imagine hundreds) unrelated results.

Perhaps there is a reason why it is worth returning everything (like for `.` only) and assuming the client (or The Client 😉) will cache it and won't call again for completion while the prefix is modified within some range.

This seems to be a separate topic so rather let's not continue it here.
